@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1787 = private unnamed_addr constant [1008 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `pytest.raises` context managers with multiple statements.\0A\0AThis rule allows `pytest.raises` bodies to contain `for`\0Aloops with empty bodies (e.g., `pass` or `...` statements), to test\0Aiterator behavior.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen a `pytest.raises` is used as a context manager and contains multiple\0Astatements, it can lead to the test passing when it actually should fail.\0A\0AA `pytest.raises` context manager should only contain a single simple\0Astatement that raises the expected exception.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0Adef test_foo():\0A    with pytest.raises(MyError):\0A        setup()\0A        func_to_test()  # not executed if `setup()` raises `MyError`\0A        assert foo()  # not executed\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0Adef test_foo():\0A    setup()\0A    with pytest.raises(MyError):\0A        func_to_test()\0A    assert foo()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`pytest` documentation: `pytest.raises`](https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/reference.html#pytest-raises)\0A", align 1
