@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1802 = private unnamed_addr constant [956 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `pytest.warns` context managers with multiple statements.\0A\0AThis rule allows `pytest.warns` bodies to contain `for`\0Aloops with empty bodies (e.g., `pass` or `...` statements), to test\0Aiterator behavior.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen `pytest.warns` is used as a context manager and contains multiple\0Astatements, it can lead to the test passing when it should instead fail.\0A\0AA `pytest.warns` context manager should only contain a single\0Asimple statement that triggers the expected warning.\0A\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0Adef test_foo_warns():\0A    with pytest.warns(Warning):\0A        setup()  # False negative if setup triggers a warning but foo does not.\0A        foo()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport pytest\0A\0A\0Adef test_foo_warns():\0A    setup()\0A    with pytest.warns(Warning):\0A        foo()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`pytest` documentation: `pytest.warns`](https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/reference.html#pytest-warns)\0A", align 1
