@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.2026 = private unnamed_addr constant [1012 x i8] c"## What it does\0ACheck for shallow `os.environ` copies.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`os.environ` is not a `dict` object, but rather, a proxy object. As such, mutating a shallow\0Acopy of `os.environ` will also mutate the original object.\0A\0ASee [BPO 15373] for more information.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport copy\0Aimport os\0A\0Aenv = copy.copy(os.environ)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport os\0A\0Aenv = os.environ.copy()\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because replacing a shallow copy with a deep copy can lead\0Ato unintended side effects. If the program modifies the shallow copy at some point, changing it\0Ato a deep copy may prevent those modifications from affecting the original data, potentially\0Aaltering the program's behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `copy` \E2\80\94 Shallow and deep copy operations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/copy.html)\0A- [Python documentation: `os.environ`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.html#os.environ)\0A\0A[BPO 15373]: https://bugs.python.org/issue15373\0A", align 1
